1
00:00:00,096 --> 00:00:02,381
You finally own the magnificent coat.

2
00:00:02,381 --> 00:00:05,406
You bought it at an auction run by your own crew,

3
00:00:05,406 --> 00:00:07,321
with most of your own share,

4
00:00:07,321 --> 00:00:09,761
after a vote you were not allowed to skip.

5
00:00:09,761 --> 00:00:11,345
Congratulations, Captain.

6
00:00:11,345 --> 00:00:13,691
The coat is the best part of the job.

7
00:00:13,691 --> 00:00:15,458
Last time, you were Tom,

8
00:00:15,458 --> 00:00:19,143
scrubbing below deck and looking up at the captain's boots.

9
00:00:19,143 --> 00:00:21,450
This time, you are wearing those boots.

10
00:00:21,450 --> 00:00:22,494
From down there,

11
00:00:22,494 --> 00:00:24,670
the job looked like freedom with a hat.

12
00:00:24,670 --> 00:00:25,898
From up here,

13
00:00:25,898 --> 00:00:28,615
it looks like everybody's complaints with a hat.

14
00:00:28,615 --> 00:00:31,058
Imagine you are an experienced seaman on

15
00:00:31,058 --> 00:00:33,352
the same modest Caribbean sloop,

16
00:00:33,352 --> 00:00:36,890
around seventeen seventeen, newly elected captain.

17
00:00:37,247 --> 00:00:37,547
You,

18
00:00:37,547 --> 00:00:40,534
this crew and this voyage are imagined.

19
00:00:40,534 --> 00:00:42,718
The problems come from trial records,

20
00:00:42,718 --> 00:00:44,155
eyewitness accounts and

21
00:00:44,155 --> 00:00:46,959
museum research about real pirate crews.

22
00:00:46,959 --> 00:00:48,993
And remember what the job was.

23
00:00:49,367 --> 00:00:52,023
A pirate captain organized robbery at sea.

24
00:00:52,023 --> 00:00:53,994
The people on the ships you chase

25
00:00:53,994 --> 00:00:54,925
are not scenery,

26
00:00:54,925 --> 00:00:58,211
even when our story keeps the violence off screen.

27
00:00:58,211 --> 00:00:59,403
Tom is still aboard,

28
00:00:59,403 --> 00:01:01,247
still attached to his bucket.

29
00:01:01,247 --> 00:01:04,057
He raised his hand for you in the vote,

30
00:01:04,057 --> 00:01:05,117
which is touching,

31
00:01:05,117 --> 00:01:07,002
until you remember that he also

32
00:01:07,002 --> 00:01:09,637
raised it to remove the last captain.

33
00:01:09,637 --> 00:01:11,027
The last captain was not

34
00:01:11,027 --> 00:01:13,058
overthrown in a sword fight.

35
00:01:13,058 --> 00:01:14,427
He was voted out.

36
00:01:14,427 --> 00:01:17,141
A heavily armed ship appeared offshore,

37
00:01:17,141 --> 00:01:18,256
he chose caution,

38
00:01:18,256 --> 00:01:20,340
and the crew decided that caution

39
00:01:20,340 --> 00:01:22,441
looked suspiciously like cowardice.

40
00:01:22,441 --> 00:01:24,045
That was not unusual.

41
00:01:24,045 --> 00:01:26,086
The economist Peter Leeson,

42
00:01:26,086 --> 00:01:29,112
in a peer-reviewed study of pirate governance,

43
00:01:29,112 --> 00:01:30,995
finds numerous examples of crews

44
00:01:30,995 --> 00:01:33,438
deposing captains by majority vote.

45
00:01:33,988 --> 00:01:35,540
In November seventeen eighteen,

46
00:01:35,540 --> 00:01:39,530
Charles Vane declined to attack a French warship.

47
00:01:39,530 --> 00:01:40,327
The next day,

48
00:01:40,327 --> 00:01:43,499
his crew voted him out and branded him a coward.

49
00:01:43,499 --> 00:01:45,874
Our main source for that story

50
00:01:45,874 --> 00:01:47,153
is Captain Charles Johnson's

51
00:01:47,153 --> 00:01:48,967
General History of the Pyrates,

52
00:01:48,967 --> 00:01:50,425
published in seventeen twenty-four.

53
00:01:50,425 --> 00:01:52,719
It needs a warning label.

54
00:01:53,077 --> 00:01:54,470
Johnson is a pen name,

55
00:01:54,470 --> 00:01:56,850
the author is still unidentified,

56
00:01:56,850 --> 00:01:59,047
and parts of the book are exaggerated.

57
00:01:59,047 --> 00:02:01,501
Johnson claims Blackbeard had fourteen wives,

58
00:02:01,501 --> 00:02:02,406
for example;

59
00:02:02,406 --> 00:02:05,019
Royal Museums Greenwich notes that the

60
00:02:05,019 --> 00:02:07,287
records show one legal marriage.

61
00:02:07,287 --> 00:02:09,218
Historians still use the book,

62
00:02:09,218 --> 00:02:13,127
carefully, alongside court records and eyewitnesses.

63
00:02:13,127 --> 00:02:13,925
So will we.

64
00:02:13,925 --> 00:02:16,705
Vane's replacement was his own quartermaster,

65
00:02:16,705 --> 00:02:17,433
John Rackham.

66
00:02:17,433 --> 00:02:19,294
That detail should worry you.

67
00:02:19,717 --> 00:02:22,342
Crews could promote the officer they trusted most,

68
00:02:22,342 --> 00:02:23,217
which means your

69
00:02:23,217 --> 00:02:25,499
successor may already be standing beside you,

70
00:02:25,499 --> 00:02:27,843
helpfully checking the water barrels.

71
00:02:27,843 --> 00:02:29,414
Your quartermaster is the same

72
00:02:29,414 --> 00:02:31,602
steady man who signed Tom aboard.

73
00:02:31,602 --> 00:02:32,353
You get along.

74
00:02:32,353 --> 00:02:35,296
You also notice that when the crew has a complaint,

75
00:02:35,296 --> 00:02:37,068
they do not bring it to you.

76
00:02:37,068 --> 00:02:38,942
They bring it to him.

77
00:02:38,942 --> 00:02:40,914
So why did they choose you?

78
00:02:40,914 --> 00:02:42,756
Johnson says crews wanted a

79
00:02:42,756 --> 00:02:45,643
captain "superior for knowledge and boldness,

80
00:02:45,643 --> 00:02:47,357
pistol proof, as they call it."

81
00:02:47,357 --> 00:02:48,799
In plain words:

82
00:02:48,799 --> 00:02:51,631
someone who can navigate and will not panic.

83
00:02:52,052 --> 00:02:54,325
Nobody asked about your management style.

84
00:02:54,325 --> 00:02:55,944
Elections could be quick.

85
00:02:55,944 --> 00:02:57,827
Johnson and the Canadian

86
00:02:57,827 --> 00:03:00,975
Encyclopedia both note that Bartholomew Roberts

87
00:03:00,975 --> 00:03:03,123
had been with his crew for only about

88
00:03:03,123 --> 00:03:05,491
six weeks when they made him captain.

89
00:03:05,491 --> 00:03:07,269
Your own campaign lasted one

90
00:03:07,269 --> 00:03:10,219
afternoon and one argument about the weather.

91
00:03:10,736 --> 00:03:13,115
Then you read the job description.

92
00:03:13,115 --> 00:03:15,545
Johnson records that a pirate

93
00:03:15,545 --> 00:03:17,531
captain's power was "absolute and

94
00:03:17,531 --> 00:03:20,020
uncontrollable" in three situations:

95
00:03:20,020 --> 00:03:22,674
"fighting, chasing, or being chased."

96
00:03:23,239 --> 00:03:26,072
Otherwise, "in all other matters whatsoever,

97
00:03:26,072 --> 00:03:27,985
he is governed by a majority."

98
00:03:27,985 --> 00:03:29,364
So you are a commander

99
00:03:29,364 --> 00:03:31,575
for the length of a chase.

100
00:03:31,575 --> 00:03:32,671
The rest of the time,

101
00:03:32,671 --> 00:03:35,529
you are a man in a good coat making suggestions.

102
00:03:35,904 --> 00:03:37,633
Where to sail, when to rest,

103
00:03:37,633 --> 00:03:39,853
how to share the food and whom to

104
00:03:39,853 --> 00:03:42,598
pursue can all end in a show of hands.

105
00:03:42,957 --> 00:03:44,128
Your orders are more

106
00:03:44,128 --> 00:03:45,521
like opening statements.

107
00:03:45,521 --> 00:03:46,893
William Snelgrave,

108
00:03:46,893 --> 00:03:48,741
the captain of a slave-trading ship,

109
00:03:48,741 --> 00:03:51,190
was captured by pirates on the West African

110
00:03:51,190 --> 00:03:53,534
coast and saw this system up close.

111
00:03:53,534 --> 00:03:54,444
The captain,

112
00:03:54,444 --> 00:03:56,961
he wrote, is "chiefly chosen to fight."

113
00:03:56,961 --> 00:03:58,236
The quartermaster,

114
00:03:58,236 --> 00:04:02,172
Snelgrave added, "has the general inspection of all affairs,

115
00:04:02,172 --> 00:04:04,416
and often controls the captain's orders."

116
00:04:04,416 --> 00:04:06,443
You read that sentence twice.

117
00:04:06,443 --> 00:04:07,803
It does not improve.

118
00:04:07,803 --> 00:04:09,228
Johnson describes the

119
00:04:09,228 --> 00:04:11,701
quartermaster as the men's own choice.

120
00:04:11,701 --> 00:04:13,407
He punished small offenses,

121
00:04:13,407 --> 00:04:16,414
settled quarrels, kept the common stores,

122
00:04:16,414 --> 00:04:19,043
and was first aboard any captured ship,

123
00:04:19,417 --> 00:04:21,673
deciding what the company would keep.

124
00:04:21,673 --> 00:04:22,965
On your sloop,

125
00:04:22,965 --> 00:04:25,666
he also decides who gets the last biscuit.

126
00:04:25,666 --> 00:04:27,231
Then there is your cabin.

127
00:04:27,231 --> 00:04:29,137
Johnson says crews gave the captain

128
00:04:29,137 --> 00:04:31,823
the great cabin, after which every man,

129
00:04:31,823 --> 00:04:34,858
"as the humour takes him," could walk in,

130
00:04:34,858 --> 00:04:36,551
use his silver and china,

131
00:04:36,551 --> 00:04:40,386
swear at him, and help himself to his food and drink.

132
00:04:40,386 --> 00:04:42,197
Your sloop is far too

133
00:04:42,197 --> 00:04:43,382
small for a great cabin.

134
00:04:43,382 --> 00:04:44,947
You have a cramped corner at the

135
00:04:44,947 --> 00:04:47,718
stern that everyone politely calls yours.

136
00:04:47,718 --> 00:04:49,919
They visit it constantly.

137
00:04:49,919 --> 00:04:52,030
The swearing fits in perfectly.

138
00:04:52,030 --> 00:04:54,453
Snelgrave noticed that on one pirate

139
00:04:54,453 --> 00:04:56,977
ship everybody slept rough on deck,

140
00:04:57,400 --> 00:05:00,385
"the captain himself not being allowed a bed."

141
00:05:00,385 --> 00:05:03,217
You had imagined a carved four-poster.

142
00:05:03,217 --> 00:05:05,266
You receive a patch of damp planks

143
00:05:05,266 --> 00:05:07,532
with an excellent view of Tom's feet.

144
00:05:07,532 --> 00:05:08,905
Next comes money.

145
00:05:09,280 --> 00:05:10,974
In the articles Johnson printed

146
00:05:10,974 --> 00:05:12,967
for Bartholomew Roberts's crew,

147
00:05:13,293 --> 00:05:15,297
the captain and the quartermaster

148
00:05:15,297 --> 00:05:17,360
each received two shares of a prize.

149
00:05:17,360 --> 00:05:18,354
The master,

150
00:05:18,354 --> 00:05:21,819
boatswain and gunner received one and a half,

151
00:05:22,202 --> 00:05:25,747
other officers one and a quarter, and everyone else one.

152
00:05:25,747 --> 00:05:27,539
Two shares sounds wonderful

153
00:05:27,539 --> 00:05:28,885
until you remember what one

154
00:05:28,885 --> 00:05:31,235
share of a modest prize looks like.

155
00:05:31,235 --> 00:05:33,322
You have doubled a disappointment.

156
00:05:33,647 --> 00:05:36,452
Leeson concludes that a pirate captain's lodging,

157
00:05:36,452 --> 00:05:38,019
food and even pay were

158
00:05:38,019 --> 00:05:40,810
nearly the same as an ordinary crewman's.

159
00:05:40,810 --> 00:05:44,065
Roberts's articles held other surprises.

160
00:05:44,065 --> 00:05:46,835
Every man had a vote in affairs of moment.

161
00:05:46,835 --> 00:05:49,746
Every man had equal title to fresh food

162
00:05:49,746 --> 00:05:52,321
and strong liquor, unless a scarcity,

163
00:05:52,321 --> 00:05:55,497
which Johnson calls "no uncommon thing among

164
00:05:55,497 --> 00:05:58,307
them," made the company vote to cut rations.

165
00:05:58,746 --> 00:06:00,738
Another clause said nobody could even

166
00:06:00,738 --> 00:06:03,119
talk of breaking up their way of living

167
00:06:03,119 --> 00:06:05,834
until each man had shared a thousand pounds.

168
00:06:06,227 --> 00:06:08,126
That is an ambitious retirement plan

169
00:06:08,126 --> 00:06:10,428
for a crew that mostly captures cloth.

170
00:06:10,866 --> 00:06:11,592
The musicians,

171
00:06:11,592 --> 00:06:14,120
meanwhile, were promised rest on the Sabbath.

172
00:06:14,494 --> 00:06:16,785
Any other day required special favor.

173
00:06:17,127 --> 00:06:18,929
Johnson admits these were only

174
00:06:18,929 --> 00:06:20,557
some of Roberts's articles,

175
00:06:20,557 --> 00:06:23,585
and no two crews wrote exactly the same rules.

176
00:06:23,585 --> 00:06:26,532
Your imagined company has its own agreement,

177
00:06:26,532 --> 00:06:28,003
but the spirit is familiar:

178
00:06:28,003 --> 00:06:31,077
the captain leads, and the crew keeps the receipts.

179
00:06:31,077 --> 00:06:33,428
Your first real test is not a battle.

180
00:06:33,428 --> 00:06:34,286
It is water.

181
00:06:34,286 --> 00:06:36,249
The barrels are low,

182
00:06:36,249 --> 00:06:40,186
the biscuit is tired, and the crew votes on where to find more.

183
00:06:40,186 --> 00:06:42,578
You suggest a quiet bay with a stream.

184
00:06:42,578 --> 00:06:44,911
Someone suggests a busy trade route.

185
00:06:44,911 --> 00:06:46,278
Thirst is persuasive,

186
00:06:46,278 --> 00:06:48,324
but treasure campaigns better.

187
00:06:48,324 --> 00:06:50,077
A pirate captain could not

188
00:06:50,077 --> 00:06:52,226
sail into most ports and order

189
00:06:52,226 --> 00:06:54,213
supplies with a polite letter.

190
00:06:54,213 --> 00:06:55,510
Food, rope,

191
00:06:55,510 --> 00:06:58,936
sailcloth and timber came from captured ships,

192
00:06:59,341 --> 00:07:03,347
or from harbors and traders willing to deal with pirates,

193
00:07:03,347 --> 00:07:05,398
for a price and for a while.

194
00:07:05,398 --> 00:07:06,970
You try the waterfront

195
00:07:06,970 --> 00:07:09,280
dealer from Tom's last visit.

196
00:07:09,280 --> 00:07:10,727
His prices have climbed,

197
00:07:10,727 --> 00:07:13,533
and so has his curiosity about your plans.

198
00:07:13,533 --> 00:07:15,084
Then there is the hull.

199
00:07:15,394 --> 00:07:16,557
Below the waterline,

200
00:07:16,557 --> 00:07:19,403
weed and barnacles grow on every wooden ship,

201
00:07:19,729 --> 00:07:21,549
and Royal Museums Greenwich notes

202
00:07:21,549 --> 00:07:23,761
that this growth could severely

203
00:07:23,761 --> 00:07:25,830
affect a sailing ship's performance.

204
00:07:25,830 --> 00:07:26,893
Britannica and the

205
00:07:26,893 --> 00:07:29,171
Smithsonian add another guest:

206
00:07:29,171 --> 00:07:32,038
shipworms, which bore into wooden hulls.

207
00:07:32,413 --> 00:07:35,089
For a pirate, speed is not a luxury.

208
00:07:35,687 --> 00:07:37,174
Johnson calls a clean,

209
00:07:37,174 --> 00:07:40,472
quick sloop "a light pair of heels," useful

210
00:07:40,472 --> 00:07:43,261
"either to take, or escape being taken."

211
00:07:43,811 --> 00:07:45,161
So you must careen:

212
00:07:45,161 --> 00:07:48,521
find a hidden beach, unload the guns and stores,

213
00:07:48,521 --> 00:07:51,562
haul the ship onto her side, and scrape.

214
00:07:51,967 --> 00:07:53,663
Careening takes days.

215
00:07:53,663 --> 00:07:55,155
For that time,

216
00:07:55,155 --> 00:07:57,824
your armed vessel is a helpless wooden whale lying

217
00:07:57,824 --> 00:08:00,138
on the sand, and your crew camps

218
00:08:00,138 --> 00:08:02,169
in tents beside everything it owns.

219
00:08:02,527 --> 00:08:04,058
Johnson describes crews being

220
00:08:04,058 --> 00:08:06,258
surprised in exactly that position.

221
00:08:06,711 --> 00:08:07,929
Stede Bonnet's whole

222
00:08:07,929 --> 00:08:09,842
career is a lesson for captains.

223
00:08:09,842 --> 00:08:11,968
A wealthy Barbados planter,

224
00:08:11,968 --> 00:08:13,655
he bought his own sloop and,

225
00:08:13,655 --> 00:08:17,924
unusually, paid his crew himself, according to both Johnson and

226
00:08:17,924 --> 00:08:20,723
North Carolina's Dictionary of Biography.

227
00:08:20,723 --> 00:08:21,784
Owning the ship did

228
00:08:21,784 --> 00:08:23,486
not keep him in charge.

229
00:08:23,486 --> 00:08:26,497
When Bonnet met Blackbeard,

230
00:08:26,497 --> 00:08:29,050
as Royal Museums Greenwich and the North Carolina

231
00:08:29,050 --> 00:08:32,085
dictionary both describe, Blackbeard put one of

232
00:08:32,085 --> 00:08:34,606
his own men in command of Bonnet's sloop.

233
00:08:35,156 --> 00:08:37,944
Bonnet himself stayed aboard Blackbeard's ship,

234
00:08:37,944 --> 00:08:38,524
somewhere

235
00:08:38,524 --> 00:08:40,733
between a guest and a prisoner.

236
00:08:40,733 --> 00:08:42,725
It is a very specific way

237
00:08:42,725 --> 00:08:45,189
to lose a job you paid for.

238
00:08:45,189 --> 00:08:46,261
In seventeen eighteen,

239
00:08:46,639 --> 00:08:50,568
Bonnet took a royal pardon, soon went back to piracy,

240
00:08:50,568 --> 00:08:52,694
and found his sloop leaking badly.

241
00:08:52,694 --> 00:08:55,077
He took her into the Cape Fear

242
00:08:55,077 --> 00:08:56,840
River to repair and scrape her hull.

243
00:08:56,840 --> 00:08:59,149
Released prisoners spread the news.

244
00:08:59,149 --> 00:09:00,691
Colonel William Rhett arrived

245
00:09:00,691 --> 00:09:02,996
with armed sloops from South Carolina.

246
00:09:03,322 --> 00:09:04,546
Bonnet surrendered and was

247
00:09:04,546 --> 00:09:06,479
hanged in Charleston that December.

248
00:09:06,933 --> 00:09:08,665
Your own careening goes well,

249
00:09:08,665 --> 00:09:10,812
mostly because nothing happens.

250
00:09:11,266 --> 00:09:14,239
You scrape beside Tom for three long days.

251
00:09:14,239 --> 00:09:16,102
He seems deeply suspicious

252
00:09:16,102 --> 00:09:18,024
of a captain holding a scraper.

253
00:09:18,024 --> 00:09:19,650
You are deeply suspicious of

254
00:09:19,650 --> 00:09:21,548
anything that might be a sail.

255
00:09:22,099 --> 00:09:22,937
Back at sea,

256
00:09:22,937 --> 00:09:25,687
you hunt, which mostly means waiting.

257
00:09:26,269 --> 00:09:27,103
Days pass.

258
00:09:27,103 --> 00:09:29,395
The crew counts biscuits,

259
00:09:29,395 --> 00:09:31,115
then counts your decisions.

260
00:09:31,115 --> 00:09:34,566
A captain without prizes is still technically employed,

261
00:09:34,566 --> 00:09:35,032
but his

262
00:09:35,032 --> 00:09:37,840
contract is renewed by show of hands.

263
00:09:37,840 --> 00:09:39,198
Then a sail appears.

264
00:09:39,198 --> 00:09:41,553
For the length of a chase,

265
00:09:41,553 --> 00:09:44,085
you finally have absolute power.

266
00:09:44,085 --> 00:09:46,571
You order the sails trimmed and

267
00:09:46,571 --> 00:09:47,680
the course changed,

268
00:09:47,680 --> 00:09:51,498
and for one glorious hour nobody votes on anything.

269
00:09:51,498 --> 00:09:53,063
The merchant sloop

270
00:09:53,063 --> 00:09:55,116
surrenders without a fight.

271
00:09:55,116 --> 00:09:58,642
That is the best outcome for everyone's survival,

272
00:09:58,642 --> 00:10:00,540
but it is still robbery.

273
00:10:00,995 --> 00:10:02,806
The sailors watching your crew climb

274
00:10:02,806 --> 00:10:05,357
aboard did nothing to deserve this afternoon.

275
00:10:05,891 --> 00:10:08,121
The quartermaster goes aboard first,

276
00:10:08,121 --> 00:10:10,740
just as Johnson and Snelgrave describe.

277
00:10:10,740 --> 00:10:13,194
He decides what the company takes.

278
00:10:13,194 --> 00:10:15,752
Your absolute power expired the

279
00:10:15,752 --> 00:10:17,647
moment the other crew gave up,

280
00:10:17,647 --> 00:10:20,944
and you are back to standing on deck in a good coat.

281
00:10:20,944 --> 00:10:24,509
Most of the prize is flour, rum and cloth.

282
00:10:24,509 --> 00:10:26,895
And one magnificent coat.

283
00:10:27,221 --> 00:10:28,323
You try it on.

284
00:10:28,323 --> 00:10:28,985
It fits.

285
00:10:28,985 --> 00:10:31,312
For one beautiful minute,

286
00:10:31,312 --> 00:10:34,034
you look exactly like the poster version of yourself.

287
00:10:34,616 --> 00:10:35,905
Snelgrave watched this

288
00:10:35,905 --> 00:10:37,250
moment happen for real.

289
00:10:37,250 --> 00:10:38,920
Several pirate captains took

290
00:10:38,920 --> 00:10:40,199
fine clothes from the plunder

291
00:10:40,199 --> 00:10:41,991
without the quartermaster's leave.

292
00:10:42,782 --> 00:10:44,521
The crew took "great offence," fearing

293
00:10:44,521 --> 00:10:46,299
that their captains would start to

294
00:10:46,299 --> 00:10:49,169
"take whatever they liked for themselves."

295
00:10:49,169 --> 00:10:50,374
The next morning,

296
00:10:50,374 --> 00:10:55,277
Snelgrave writes, the coats were taken back and put into the common chest,

297
00:10:55,277 --> 00:10:56,634
"to be sold at the mast."

298
00:10:56,634 --> 00:10:59,214
Leeson treats the episode as evidence of

299
00:10:59,214 --> 00:11:01,732
how tightly crews limited their captains.

300
00:11:01,732 --> 00:11:02,958
Your quartermaster

301
00:11:02,958 --> 00:11:04,700
has never read Snelgrave.

302
00:11:04,700 --> 00:11:06,068
He does not need to.

303
00:11:06,068 --> 00:11:07,503
He holds out his hand.

304
00:11:07,503 --> 00:11:09,617
The coat goes into the chest,

305
00:11:09,617 --> 00:11:11,092
then up for auction.

306
00:11:11,092 --> 00:11:13,058
You outbid two crewmates and,

307
00:11:13,058 --> 00:11:14,939
for some reason, Tom.

308
00:11:14,939 --> 00:11:16,553
You did not seize the coat.

309
00:11:16,553 --> 00:11:18,605
You bought it back from the voters.

310
00:11:18,605 --> 00:11:20,016
The captured sailors watch

311
00:11:20,016 --> 00:11:21,768
all this from their own deck.

312
00:11:21,768 --> 00:11:23,554
Some may be invited to join,

313
00:11:23,554 --> 00:11:24,839
some pressured.

314
00:11:25,228 --> 00:11:26,823
Whatever your crew decides,

315
00:11:26,823 --> 00:11:28,191
they do not get a vote.

316
00:11:28,191 --> 00:11:30,332
The fairness aboard a pirate

317
00:11:30,332 --> 00:11:32,007
ship stopped at its own rail.

318
00:11:32,007 --> 00:11:33,235
Enslaved people stood

319
00:11:33,235 --> 00:11:35,356
even further outside it.

320
00:11:35,356 --> 00:11:38,883
Royal Museums Greenwich says that,

321
00:11:38,883 --> 00:11:40,373
despite some stories of pirates

322
00:11:40,373 --> 00:11:41,986
freeing enslaved people,

323
00:11:41,986 --> 00:11:45,778
this rarely happened, and pirates often treated enslaved

324
00:11:45,778 --> 00:11:47,891
people as badly as their captors did.

325
00:11:48,330 --> 00:11:50,045
Blackbeard's famous flagship,

326
00:11:50,045 --> 00:11:51,719
Queen Anne's Revenge,

327
00:11:51,719 --> 00:11:54,816
began as La Concorde, a French slave ship

328
00:11:54,816 --> 00:11:56,868
taken in November seventeen seventeen.

329
00:11:57,486 --> 00:11:59,395
According to the Smithsonian and North

330
00:11:59,395 --> 00:12:02,434
Carolina's Department of Natural and Cultural Resources,

331
00:12:02,434 --> 00:12:04,155
most of the captives aboard

332
00:12:04,155 --> 00:12:07,086
were carried on to Martinique to be sold.

333
00:12:07,086 --> 00:12:09,562
A change of flag was not freedom.

334
00:12:09,562 --> 00:12:10,686
Some men of African

335
00:12:10,686 --> 00:12:13,078
descent did serve as pirate crew.

336
00:12:13,078 --> 00:12:15,110
But how pirates decided who counted

337
00:12:15,110 --> 00:12:17,852
as crew and who was treated as cargo,

338
00:12:17,852 --> 00:12:20,797
the Smithsonian notes, remains unclear.

339
00:12:21,412 --> 00:12:22,548
Your crew talks a

340
00:12:22,548 --> 00:12:24,252
great deal about fairness.

341
00:12:24,252 --> 00:12:25,933
History asks who was never

342
00:12:25,933 --> 00:12:27,942
allowed into that conversation.

343
00:12:28,316 --> 00:12:29,748
Back aboard your sloop,

344
00:12:29,748 --> 00:12:33,632
morale is excellent for exactly as long as the rum lasts.

345
00:12:33,632 --> 00:12:36,660
Then someone asks loudly why the

346
00:12:36,660 --> 00:12:38,536
captain always chooses the slow route.

347
00:12:38,536 --> 00:12:39,594
Someone else asks why

348
00:12:39,594 --> 00:12:41,352
the coat sold so cheaply.

349
00:12:41,352 --> 00:12:42,711
You begin to understand how

350
00:12:42,711 --> 00:12:45,078
quickly a vote can become a verdict.

351
00:12:45,078 --> 00:12:47,129
Removal was not always polite.

352
00:12:47,438 --> 00:12:48,657
Snelgrave heard that

353
00:12:48,657 --> 00:12:50,980
Christopher Moody's crew forced him and

354
00:12:50,980 --> 00:12:52,574
twelve others into an open boat,

355
00:12:52,574 --> 00:12:54,733
and they were never heard of again.

356
00:12:55,203 --> 00:12:56,523
One pirate's advice,

357
00:12:56,523 --> 00:13:00,134
according to Johnson, was simple: if a captain ever

358
00:13:00,134 --> 00:13:02,516
exceeded his limits, "why, down with him!"

359
00:13:03,002 --> 00:13:04,303
So you do what sensible

360
00:13:04,303 --> 00:13:07,055
pirate captains did: you consult.

361
00:13:07,055 --> 00:13:08,354
About the course,

362
00:13:08,354 --> 00:13:11,353
the rations, the weather and the dinner.

363
00:13:11,353 --> 00:13:14,745
You become the most democratic man in the Caribbean,

364
00:13:14,745 --> 00:13:15,862
mainly out of a strong

365
00:13:15,862 --> 00:13:18,777
preference for not being placed in an open boat.

366
00:13:18,777 --> 00:13:19,959
Then the dealer

367
00:13:19,959 --> 00:13:22,030
brings news from the harbor.

368
00:13:22,030 --> 00:13:23,794
On the fifth of September,

369
00:13:23,794 --> 00:13:25,645
seventeen seventeen, King George the First

370
00:13:25,645 --> 00:13:28,047
issued a proclamation against pirates.

371
00:13:28,502 --> 00:13:30,011
Any pirate who surrendered to a

372
00:13:30,011 --> 00:13:31,855
governor by the fifth of September,

373
00:13:31,855 --> 00:13:32,386
seventeen eighteen,

374
00:13:32,386 --> 00:13:36,642
would be pardoned for piracies committed before the

375
00:13:36,642 --> 00:13:38,478
fifth of January, seventeen eighteen.

376
00:13:38,916 --> 00:13:41,094
Johnson prints the proclamation in full,

377
00:13:41,094 --> 00:13:42,975
and the Calendar of State

378
00:13:42,975 --> 00:13:45,044
Papers records the planning behind it.

379
00:13:45,044 --> 00:13:46,474
After the deadline,

380
00:13:46,474 --> 00:13:49,309
it promised rewards for capturing those who refused.

381
00:13:49,908 --> 00:13:52,285
One hundred pounds for a commander.

382
00:13:52,285 --> 00:13:54,450
Forty for officers such

383
00:13:54,450 --> 00:13:56,400
as the master or gunner.

384
00:13:56,400 --> 00:13:58,180
Thirty for lesser officers.

385
00:13:58,180 --> 00:14:00,462
And twenty for an ordinary man.

386
00:14:00,997 --> 00:14:02,601
There was one more clause.

387
00:14:02,601 --> 00:14:04,383
Any pirate who seized his own

388
00:14:04,383 --> 00:14:06,471
commander and delivered him to

389
00:14:06,471 --> 00:14:08,421
justice would receive two hundred pounds.

390
00:14:08,421 --> 00:14:10,396
The State Papers sum it

391
00:14:10,396 --> 00:14:11,891
up as "double the reward."

392
00:14:11,891 --> 00:14:14,493
You do the arithmetic, and so does Tom.

393
00:14:14,493 --> 00:14:15,729
Tom is worth twenty pounds.

394
00:14:16,386 --> 00:14:17,714
You are worth one hundred.

395
00:14:17,714 --> 00:14:20,411
If Tom hands you over himself,

396
00:14:20,411 --> 00:14:21,499
he earns two hundred.

397
00:14:21,499 --> 00:14:23,521
Your promotion made you five

398
00:14:23,521 --> 00:14:24,996
times more valuable,

399
00:14:24,996 --> 00:14:28,199
and the money goes to everybody except you.

400
00:14:28,199 --> 00:14:29,910
Tom notices you noticing.

401
00:14:29,910 --> 00:14:31,095
He returns to his

402
00:14:31,095 --> 00:14:33,061
bucket with great dignity.

403
00:14:33,061 --> 00:14:35,271
You decide to sleep facing the hatch.

404
00:14:35,789 --> 00:14:36,413
At Nassau,

405
00:14:36,413 --> 00:14:39,665
the pirates' favorite base, the news caused uproar.

406
00:14:40,054 --> 00:14:41,904
The London Gazette reported that the

407
00:14:41,904 --> 00:14:43,854
proclamation was received "with great

408
00:14:43,854 --> 00:14:46,249
demonstration of joy and thankfulness."

409
00:14:46,249 --> 00:14:48,682
Johnson describes a pirate council

410
00:14:48,682 --> 00:14:51,110
so noisy that nothing could be agreed.

411
00:14:51,110 --> 00:14:52,229
Both may be true.

412
00:14:52,229 --> 00:14:53,908
Some captains surrendered,

413
00:14:53,908 --> 00:14:56,080
including Benjamin Hornigold.

414
00:14:56,080 --> 00:14:58,197
In the summer of seventeen eighteen,

415
00:14:58,654 --> 00:15:00,588
Woodes Rogers arrived as governor,

416
00:15:00,588 --> 00:15:03,067
with the pardon and naval escorts.

417
00:15:03,067 --> 00:15:05,229
Most of the pirates there accepted.

418
00:15:05,229 --> 00:15:07,096
Charles Vane did not.

419
00:15:07,096 --> 00:15:08,893
He set fire to a captured ship,

420
00:15:08,893 --> 00:15:10,574
sent it toward the warships,

421
00:15:10,574 --> 00:15:12,840
and escaped in the confusion.

422
00:15:12,840 --> 00:15:14,801
The pardon was not a magic door.

423
00:15:14,801 --> 00:15:16,494
Johnson says many who took

424
00:15:16,494 --> 00:15:18,736
it soon returned to the trade.

425
00:15:18,736 --> 00:15:19,899
Blackbeard accepted a

426
00:15:19,899 --> 00:15:21,432
pardon from North Carolina's

427
00:15:21,432 --> 00:15:23,577
governor and did not stay retired.

428
00:15:23,577 --> 00:15:26,284
At Nassau, when some pardoned men went

429
00:15:26,284 --> 00:15:28,976
back to robbery, Rogers had them tried and

430
00:15:28,976 --> 00:15:31,581
hanged in front of their old companions.

431
00:15:31,581 --> 00:15:34,581
Meanwhile, the hunters were improving.

432
00:15:34,581 --> 00:15:36,174
On the twenty-second of November,

433
00:15:36,174 --> 00:15:36,907
seventeen eighteen,

434
00:15:36,907 --> 00:15:41,349
an expedition sent by Virginia's governor and

435
00:15:41,349 --> 00:15:43,073
led by Lieutenant Robert Maynard

436
00:15:43,073 --> 00:15:45,465
caught Blackbeard at Ocracoke Inlet.

437
00:15:45,465 --> 00:15:47,670
Blackbeard did not survive the fight.

438
00:15:48,045 --> 00:15:49,589
Bartholomew Roberts,

439
00:15:49,589 --> 00:15:53,392
Royal Museums Greenwich says, took more than four

440
00:15:53,392 --> 00:15:55,460
hundred vessels in about three years.

441
00:15:55,899 --> 00:15:57,972
On the tenth of February,

442
00:15:57,972 --> 00:16:00,408
seventeen twenty-two, the warship Swallow,

443
00:16:00,408 --> 00:16:03,516
under Captain Chaloner Ogle, caught him off West Africa.

444
00:16:04,017 --> 00:16:05,778
Roberts was killed in the battle.

445
00:16:05,778 --> 00:16:06,969
Capture did not mean a

446
00:16:06,969 --> 00:16:08,268
long voyage to London.

447
00:16:08,268 --> 00:16:09,908
Admiralty courts sat in the

448
00:16:09,908 --> 00:16:11,853
colonies and trading forts,

449
00:16:11,853 --> 00:16:13,652
close to where pirates were caught.

450
00:16:13,652 --> 00:16:15,218
After Roberts's death,

451
00:16:15,218 --> 00:16:19,465
fifty-two of his men were hanged following a trial at Cape Coast Castle,

452
00:16:19,465 --> 00:16:21,228
on the West African coast.

453
00:16:21,228 --> 00:16:21,620
Vane,

454
00:16:21,620 --> 00:16:24,692
too, was eventually caught and hanged in Jamaica.

455
00:16:24,692 --> 00:16:26,751
The famous careers were short.

456
00:16:27,092 --> 00:16:29,055
Royal Museums Greenwich says

457
00:16:29,055 --> 00:16:31,601
people who entered piracy usually

458
00:16:31,601 --> 00:16:33,905
did so only for a short period.

459
00:16:33,905 --> 00:16:35,874
Roberts lasted about three years.

460
00:16:35,874 --> 00:16:38,025
Blackbeard's career as a pirate

461
00:16:38,025 --> 00:16:40,077
leader lasted only a few years.

462
00:16:40,077 --> 00:16:42,503
Johnson gives Roberts a motto:

463
00:16:42,503 --> 00:16:45,431
"A merry life and a short one."

464
00:16:45,431 --> 00:16:48,174
Whatever else in that book was exaggerated,

465
00:16:48,174 --> 00:16:50,353
the short part was reliable.

466
00:16:50,353 --> 00:16:51,463
Now comes the vote.

467
00:16:51,463 --> 00:16:52,741
Before the deadline,

468
00:16:52,741 --> 00:16:54,921
your crew gathers on deck to decide:

469
00:16:54,921 --> 00:16:57,298
take the pardon, or keep sailing.

470
00:16:57,298 --> 00:17:00,115
As captain, you get to speak first.

471
00:17:00,115 --> 00:17:01,486
Then the hands go up.

472
00:17:01,486 --> 00:17:02,671
You have one vote.

473
00:17:02,671 --> 00:17:04,042
So does Tom.

474
00:17:04,042 --> 00:17:06,432
The quartermaster counts slowly.

475
00:17:06,432 --> 00:17:08,225
The real crews of seventeen eighteen

476
00:17:08,225 --> 00:17:09,920
split every possible way:

477
00:17:09,920 --> 00:17:14,204
some surrendered, some went back, some never had a real choice.

478
00:17:14,204 --> 00:17:16,529
Your imagined count can wait.

479
00:17:16,529 --> 00:17:18,287
Whatever it says, you will hear it

480
00:17:18,287 --> 00:17:20,701
at the same moment as everybody else.

481
00:17:20,701 --> 00:17:22,024
So why was being a pirate

482
00:17:22,024 --> 00:17:23,852
captain worse than you think?

483
00:17:24,242 --> 00:17:26,745
Because your power lasted as long as a chase,

484
00:17:26,745 --> 00:17:28,617
your cabin belonged to everyone,

485
00:17:28,617 --> 00:17:31,408
and your share was two pieces of not very much.

486
00:17:31,943 --> 00:17:33,849
Because your crew could vote you out,

487
00:17:33,849 --> 00:17:35,151
and your own king priced

488
00:17:35,151 --> 00:17:37,119
you at five times your friends.

489
00:17:37,119 --> 00:17:38,679
But the coat is yours.

490
00:17:38,679 --> 00:17:39,959
You paid for it.

491
00:17:39,959 --> 00:17:42,328
Twice, if you count the job.

492
00:17:42,328 --> 00:17:44,363
If you want to see which life behind

493
00:17:44,363 --> 00:17:46,761
the legend gets worse next time, subscribe.

494
00:17:47,103 --> 00:17:48,481
This is Pirate Pages:

495
00:17:48,481 --> 00:17:50,759
the lives behind the legends.
